    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1863\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the private housing finance law, in relation to\nlimited profit housing companies in a city with a population of one\nmillion or more\n\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nThis bill would amend the Private Housing Finance Law by adding a new\nsection 35-a thereto. Section 35-a. This would provide that in cities\nwith a population of one million or more, rents for housing\naccommodations in rental developments leaving the Mitchell-Lama\nprogram that are governed post-dissolution by the rent stabilization\nlaws will be the last rent authorized for the affected dwelling unit\nbefore the dissolution date and shall not at any time be subject to\nadjustment pursuant to subdivision (a) of section 26-513 of the New\nYork City Administrative Code (the provision allowing the adjustment\nof initial legal regulated rent under \"unique and peculiar\ncircumstances\").\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nUnder Private Housing Finance Law § 35, Mitchell-Lama housing\ncompanies may voluntarily dissolve (or \"buyout\") after 20 years. Many\nhousing companies are opting to buyout in order to increase the return\non their investments beyond the current Mitchell-Lama statutory\nlimits.\n\nThis bill is intended to protect tenants of Mitchell-Lama developments\nin New York City that were operated as rentals before exiting the\nprogram and that will be subject to rent stabilization laws after the\ndissolution, by protecting the tenants from potentially large rent\nincreases as a result of Administrative Code § 26-5l3(a), which allows\nincreases of initial legal regulated rents when there are \"unique and\npeculiar circumstances\" resulting in a rent that is substantially\ndifferent than the rents prevailing in substantially similar housing\naccommodations in the same area. By providing that these tenants'\ninitial legal regulated rents on or after the dissolution date shall\nbe the last rents authorized for their respective dwelling units\nbefore the dissolution date of the Mitchell-Lama development, and by\nexpressly prohibiting any upward adjustments under the \"unique and\npeculiar circumstances\" provision, this bill would maintain continued\naffordability for tenants residing in their units. In essence,\naffected tenants' initial rents would be comparable to what they are\npaying now, and the owner would be able to get the normal Rent\nGuidelines Board increases on future leases. This bill would thereby\navoid the potential displacement of many tenants who might otherwise\nnot be able to afford their rents.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008 - A.352 - Passed the Assembly\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
